30
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:26,120
Well, at the beginning of today's dialogue, Jonathan said, I'm sure many of you will be keen to ask some questions.

31
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:28,200
What does this word keen mean?

32
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:28,700
All right.

33
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:35,340
So to be keen to do something, which basically means that you want to or would like to do something.

34
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:36,060
Right.

35
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:36,760
Are you keen?

36
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:39,540
Would you like to do it? Are you interested in doing it?

37
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:45,480
Now, this is a very British form of addressing something, right?

38
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:46,370
That's right. This is very British.

39
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:48,970
We don't really say this as much in America, but you can.

40
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:50,650
People will understand you.

41
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:56,970
I would say, I'm sure many of you would like to ask some questions and so you could say that both ways.

42
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:57,810
That's right.

43
00:02:58,110 --> 00:02:58,410
That's right.

44
00:02:58,950 --> 00:02:59,330
Okay.

45
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:08,410
So many people want to ask questions and Jonathan said, so I'd like to open it up to a Q&A session.

46
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:13,420
So the series of words here, to open it up to a Q&A session. What does that mean?

47
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:14,720
To open it up.

48
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,940
It means the discussion. So he would like to invite people to ask questions.

49
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:25,760
So I'd like to open it up for questions mean I would like to give you guys the opportunity to ask.

50
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:31,220
